## Phase 1: Preparation

### 1-1: Investigate the changelogs of dependent libraries

Record in `00-analysis/library-changelog-review.md`:
- Identify libraries with a major version difference (CP-1)
- Extract behavioral changes from CHANGES/NEWS
- **Enumerate newly added compatibility options/flags** (= a signal of a behavioral change)

See: `00-analysis/analysis-appendix.md` and the Playbook material under `docs/reference/`

### 1-2: Capture the baseline (the mode choice is an explicit decision)

**Fix the mode first and state it in the work-plan** (details: `02-test/baseline-behavior.md`.
Combining modes is allowed):
- **Mode A (measure on the old environment)**: when the old environment is available. Record correct
  behavior by observing the real system into `02-test/test-results/baseline/` (CP-5). This can only be
  captured while the old environment is alive. Do it first
- **Mode B (define by reading the code)**: when the old environment is unavailable. Define the expected
  behavior from code reading in `02-test/baseline-behavior.md`. **Always state the evidence (source:line)
  and the confidence for each item**, and note the low baseline confidence in the work-plan's risk section
- **Mode C (reuse the existing tests)**: when existing test assets are available. Use the existing
  unit/E2E test suite as the baseline.
  **Count migrating the test infrastructure itself (runner, drivers, execution environment) as a
  Step in the work-plan**

**For Mode B (define by reading the code), the coverage baseline belongs in the file set of the primary source code.** Do not use an AI analysis result as the specification (what was never written stays missing from the baseline and nobody notices; CP-3). Include not only source files but also views, templates and configuration files — platform coupling concentrates there.

### 1-3: Decide the scope of the non-functional requirements [mandatory, Tier 2 = HOLD]

For each item below, **decide "in scope" or "out of scope" with a reason and record it as an ADR**.
This is a scope decision (ADR-level), so **user approval is required (HOLD-7)**.
You must not proceed into the implementation phase with an "implicitly out of scope" item.

| Item | What to decide | Where it is implemented if in scope |
|------|----------------|-------------------------------------|
| Performance | Whether to compare response time / throughput against the baseline | `./verify.sh nonfunc` (perf) |
| Resource leaks | Whether to run long-running tests with a leak detection tool (valgrind, profiler, etc.) | `./verify.sh nonfunc` (leak) |
| Fault tolerance | Whether to run failure-path and restart tests | `02-test/integration/` |
| Security | Whether to check for and fix remaining known vulnerability patterns | The in/out of scope table in the work-plan |

- Items put **in scope**: register them in the work-plan's success criteria table with a
  verification method, and enable verify.sh's nonfunc gate
- Items put **out of scope**: keep them in the success criteria table as a row reading
  "out of scope (see ADR-N)" (never make being out of scope invisible)

### Phase 1 completion conditions

- [ ] The version differences and behavioral changes of dependent libraries are recorded
- [ ] The baseline mode is explicitly chosen, and the baseline is captured/defined
- [ ] **In/out of scope is recorded as an ADR for every non-functional item, and user-approved**
- [ ] `03-worklog/session-context.md` and `worklog.md` are updated

